::ALG1_U06_L01_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
The graphs of y = x + 3 and y = -x + 7 intersect at (2, 5). What is the solution?
{
=A. (2, 5)#Correct. The solution is the intersection point.
~B. (5, 2)#This reverses the coordinates.
~C. x = 2#This gives only one coordinate.
~D. y = 5#This gives only one coordinate.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which point satisfies both y = x + 1 and y = 2x - 2?
{
~A. (1, 2)#This works in y = x + 1 but not in y = 2x - 2.
~B. (2, 4)#This works in y = 2x - 2 but not in y = x + 1.
=C. (3, 4)#Correct. 4 = 3 + 1 and 4 = 2(3) - 2.
~D. (4, 3)#This reverses the coordinates.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
What is true about y = 4x + 2 and y = 4x - 6?
{
~A. The system has one solution#Same slope and different intercepts means the lines do not cross.
=B. The system has no solution#Correct. The lines are parallel.
~C. The system has infinitely many solutions#The lines are not identical because the intercepts differ.
~D. The solution is (0, 2)#That point is only on the first line.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Two equations graph as the same line. How many solutions does the system have?
{
~A. No solution#No solution happens with parallel distinct lines.
~B. Exactly one solution#One solution happens when lines cross once.
~C. Exactly two solutions#Two different lines cannot intersect at exactly two points.
=D. Infinitely many solutions#Correct. Every point on the shared line satisfies both equations.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which ordered pair is the solution to the system y = -x + 8 and y = x + 2?
{
=A. (3, 5)#Correct. Both equations give y = 5 when x = 3.
~B. (5, 3)#This reverses the coordinates and does not satisfy both equations.
~C. (0, 8)#This is the y-intercept of one line only.
~D. (0, 2)#This is the y-intercept of one line only.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q06::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q06] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
On a graph, two lines cross at (-1, 4). What should be reported as the solution?
{
~A. x = -1#This is only the x-coordinate.
=B. (-1, 4)#Correct. The solution must include both coordinates.
~C. (4, -1)#This reverses x and y.
~D. y = 4#This is only the y-coordinate.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q07::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q07] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which point satisfies both y = 3x - 1 and y = x + 5?
{
~A. (1, 2)#This works in y = 3x - 1 but not in y = x + 5.
~B. (2, 7)#This works in y = x + 5 but not in y = 3x - 1.
=C. (3, 8)#Correct. 8 = 3(3) - 1 and 8 = 3 + 5.
~D. (8, 3)#This reverses the coordinates.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q08::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q08] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Two lines have slopes 2 and -1. What does that tell you before graphing?
{
~A. The lines must be parallel#Parallel lines have equal slopes.
~B. The system has no solution#Different slopes mean the lines will intersect once.
~C. The equations are identical#Identical lines have the same slope and intercept.
=D. The system has one solution#Correct. Lines with different slopes intersect at exactly one point.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q09::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q09] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which system has no solution?
{
=A. y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x - 3#Correct. Same slope and different intercepts create parallel lines.
~B. y = 2x + 1 and y = -2x + 1#Different slopes create one intersection.
~C. y = x and y = x#These are identical and have infinitely many solutions.
~D. y = x + 1 and y = 3x - 5#Different slopes create one solution.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q10::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q10] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which system has infinitely many solutions?
{
~A. y = x + 4 and y = -x + 4#Different slopes create one solution.
=B. y = 2x + 6 and 2y = 4x + 12#Correct. The second equation simplifies to y = 2x + 6.
~C. y = 5x - 2 and y = 5x + 3#Same slope but different intercepts creates no solution.
~D. y = -3x and y = 3x#Different slopes create one solution.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q11::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q11] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
The point (2, 7) lies on both lines in a system. What does that mean?
{
~A. The system has no solution#A shared point is a solution.
~B. The point is only the y-intercept#A point with x = 2 is not a y-intercept.
=C. (2, 7) is the solution#Correct. A point on both lines satisfies both equations.
~D. The slopes must be equal#Lines can share one point and have different slopes.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q12::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q12] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which ordered pair satisfies y = -2x + 9 and y = x?
{
~A. (1, 1)#This gives -2(1) + 9 = 7, not 1.
~B. (2, 2)#This gives -2(2) + 9 = 5, not 2.
~C. (4, 4)#This gives -2(4) + 9 = 1, not 4.
=D. (3, 3)#Correct. -2(3) + 9 = 3.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q13::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q13] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
A system's graph shows parallel lines. Which statement is true?
{
=A. There is no ordered pair that satisfies both equations#Correct. Parallel lines do not intersect.
~B. There is exactly one solution#One solution requires one intersection.
~C. Every point on either line is a solution#That only happens when the lines are identical.
~D. The solution is the y-intercept#The y-intercepts are different points on different lines.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q14::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q14] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which point is on both y = x - 2 and y = -x + 4?
{
~A. (0, -2)#This is only on y = x - 2.
=B. (3, 1)#Correct. 1 = 3 - 2 and 1 = -3 + 4.
~C. (1, 3)#This reverses the solution coordinates.
~D. (4, 0)#This is only on y = -x + 4.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q15::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q15] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
What should you do after reading an intersection point from a graph?
{
~A. Use only the x-coordinate as the answer#A system solution needs both coordinates.
~B. Round both coordinates automatically#Rounding is not needed unless the graph is approximate.
=C. Check the ordered pair in both equations#Correct. Checking confirms the point satisfies the system.
~D. Change both equations to have the same slope#Changing the equations changes the system.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q16::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q16] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Two gym plans are y = 20 + 10x and y = 50 + 5x. The graphs intersect at (6, 80). What does 6 represent?
{
~A. The total cost in dollars#The y-coordinate gives the total cost.
~B. The monthly fee for both plans#The equations have different monthly rates.
~C. The starting cost#The starting costs are the y-intercepts, 20 and 50.
=D. The number of months when the costs are equal#Correct. The x-coordinate represents months.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q17::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q17] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which ordered pair satisfies both y = 4x and y = x + 6?
{
=A. (2, 8)#Correct. 8 = 4(2) and 8 = 2 + 6.
~B. (8, 2)#This reverses the coordinates.
~C. (0, 6)#This is on y = x + 6 but not on y = 4x.
~D. (1, 4)#This is on y = 4x but not on y = x + 6.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q18::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q18] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which statement best describes the solution of y = -x + 3 and y = -x + 3?
{
~A. No solution#The equations are the same line, not parallel distinct lines.
=B. Infinitely many solutions#Correct. Every point on the line satisfies both equations.
~C. One solution at (0, 3)#That point is one solution, but not the only one.
~D. One solution at (3, 0)#That point is one solution, but not the only one.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q19::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q19] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which system has one solution?
{
~A. y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x + 1#Identical lines have infinitely many solutions.
~B. y = -3x + 4 and y = -3x - 2#Same slope and different intercepts have no solution.
=C. y = x + 5 and y = -2x - 1#Correct. Different slopes create exactly one intersection.
~D. y = 4x and y = 4x#Identical lines have infinitely many solutions.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q20::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q20] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
The graphs of two equations intersect at (0, -2). Which statement is true?
{
~A. x = -2 and y = 0#This reverses the coordinates.
~B. Only one equation must contain the point#A system solution must satisfy both equations.
~C. The lines are parallel#Parallel lines do not intersect.
=D. x = 0 and y = -2 satisfy both equations#Correct. The intersection point belongs to both lines.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q21::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q21] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which point satisfies y = 2x + 3 and y = 5x - 6?
{
=A. (3, 9)#Correct. Both equations give y = 9 when x = 3.
~B. (9, 3)#This reverses the coordinates.
~C. (0, 3)#This is only on y = 2x + 3.
~D. (2, 7)#This works in y = 2x + 3 but not in y = 5x - 6.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q22::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q22] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Why is (1, 4) the solution to y = 3x + 1 and y = x + 3?
{
~A. It satisfies only the first equation#It also satisfies the second equation because 4 = 1 + 3.
~B. It satisfies only the second equation#It also satisfies the first equation because 4 = 3(1) + 1.
=C. It satisfies both equations#Correct. Substitution makes both equations true.
~D. It is the y-intercept of both lines#The point has x = 1, so it is not a y-intercept.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q23::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q23] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which point is the solution to y = 2x - 4 and y = -x + 5?
{
~A. (1, -2)#This is on y = 2x - 4 but not on y = -x + 5.
=B. (3, 2)#Correct. 2 = 2(3) - 4 and 2 = -3 + 5.
~C. (2, 3)#This reverses the coordinates.
~D. (0, 5)#This is only on y = -x + 5.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q24::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q24] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Two lines have the same y-intercept but different slopes. What must be true?
{
~A. They have no solution#They meet at the shared y-intercept.
~B. They have infinitely many solutions#Different slopes mean they are not the same line.
~C. They are parallel#Parallel lines must have the same slope.
=D. They have exactly one solution#Correct. They intersect once at the shared y-intercept.
}

::ALG1_U06_L01_Q25::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_Q25] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
What is the solution type for y = -2x + 4 and 2y = -4x + 8?
{
=A. Infinitely many solutions#Correct. The second equation simplifies to y = -2x + 4.
~B. No solution#No solution would require parallel distinct lines.
~C. One solution at (0, 4)#This is one point on the line, but not the only solution.
~D. One solution at (4, 0)#This is one point on the line, but not the only solution.
}
